",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"systemd unit inventory-app.service is active with python3 bound to port 8181; ss confirms LISTEN on *:8181; curl against $NODE_IP:8181 returns the JSON inventory payload and /health returns 'ok', both before and after the restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2a719c93405248d1\",\"cmd-5c86ee95e2004281\",\"cmd-a8c6b8130b734054\",\"cmd-58b870bb5ef14a33\"]},{\"id\":\"mac-enforcing-mode\",\"requirement\":\"System's mandatory access control (AppArmor) remains in enforcing mode while the app is served\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"aa-status shows AppArmor loaded with all profiles (13 then 14, including inventory-app) in enforce mode both before deployment and while the service is running; /proc/<pid>/attr/current shows the serving process labeled 'inventory-app (enforce)'. Enforcing status is also confirmed post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-749da2b09a074dcb\",\"cmd-18f839b02f014259\",\"cmd-5c86ee95e2004281\",\"cmd-58b870bb5ef14a33\"]},{\"id\":\"mac-genuine-enforcement\",\"requirement\":\"MAC confinement of the service is genuine, not decorative\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Two launch attempts under the loaded profile were actually blocked (PermissionError reading /etc/mime.types before the profile was widened), and dmesg shows real kernel AUDIT DENIED entries scoped to the inventory-app profile for the running pid, demonstrating live enforcement rather than a no-op or complain-mode stub.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6e6f893a458646d9\",\"cmd-9492547369c74bfb\",\"cmd-c6ddf15fded54855\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Command trace reviewed chronologically. Most state-changing commands map directly to the task: creating /opt/inventory-app and the app file, creating the dedicated 'inventory' system user, authoring and loading the AppArmor profile (initial version plus one corrective reload after the mime.types denial), creating/enabling the systemd unit, and the final restart cycle. Two manual out-of-systemd launches (cmd-6e6f893a458646d9, cmd-9492547369c74bfb) were troubleshooting attempts that crashed on their own (permission error, then a Python 3.5 API incompatibility) rather than being cleanly reverted by the executor, but they were bounded (single foreground process each, no residue, no listening socket left behind) and their AppArmor denials were reused as required evidence of genuine enforcement, so they are not purely wasted actions. The one systemd-managed start that hit start-limit-hit (cmd-513f96a732534486) was corrected via systemctl reset-failed and a subsequent successful start, leaving no failed unit behind (after_executor failed-services list is empty). No unrelated packages, files, or listening sockets remain outside what the task requires. Lifecycle diff shows only the expected boot-id change and systemd-private tmp directories from the reboot itself, no executor-caused residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6e6f893a458646d9\",\"cmd-9492547369c74bfb\",\"cmd-513f96a732534486\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task—serving the inventory app on TCP 8181, keeping AppArmor in enforcing mode, and proving that enforcement is real rather than decorative—are directly and coherently evidenced by systemd status, ss, curl, aa-status, /proc attr, and dmesg audit output. The separately-required node-restart evidence protocol is also fully demonstrated: a pre-restart healthy baseline (old boot_id, enforcing AppArmor, active service, working HTTP) is followed by a reboot acknowledgement, an observed unreachable period, and a post-restart snapshot showing a new boot_id together with recovered AppArmor enforcement, systemd service state, and HTTP responses. Hygiene is good overall; two failed manual debugging launches were bounded and contributed evidence toward the genuine-enforcement requirement rather than being pure waste.\",\"limitations\":[\"The two manual foreground launch attempts (cmd-6e6f893a458646d9, cmd-9492547369c74bfb) failed on their own rather than being deliberately stopped/cleaned by the executor; the trace does not show an explicit teardown command for them, though no residue (process or socket) was observed afterward.\",\"Boot-time evidence relies on 'who -b' and boot_id together; no independent third-party timestamp source was available to cross-check the claimed restart window beyond the trace itself.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"port-8181-serving","requirement":"Inventory application serves over TCP port 8181 on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"systemd unit inventory-app.service is active with python3 bound to port 8181; ss confirms LISTEN on *:8181; curl against $NODE_IP:8181 returns the JSON inventory payload and /health returns 'ok', both before and after the restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2a719c93405248d1","cmd-5c86ee95e2004281","cmd-a8c6b8130b734054","cmd-58b870bb5ef14a33"]},{"id":"mac-enforcing-mode","requirement":"System's mandatory access control (AppArmor) remains in enforcing mode while the app is served","status":"satisfied","summary":"aa-status shows AppArmor loaded with all profiles (13 then 14, including inventory-app) in enforce mode both before deployment and while the service is running; /proc/<pid>/attr/current shows the serving process labeled 'inventory-app (enforce)'. Enforcing status is also confirmed post-restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-749da2b09a074dcb","cmd-18f839b02f014259","cmd-5c86ee95e2004281","cmd-58b870bb5ef14a33"]},{"id":"mac-genuine-enforcement","requirement":"MAC confinement of the service is genuine, not decorative","status":"satisfied","summary":"Two launch attempts under the loaded profile were actually blocked (PermissionError reading /etc/mime.types before the profile was widened), and dmesg shows real kernel AUDIT DENIED entries scoped to the inventory-app profile for the running pid, dem